\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{An orbit on the n-nullcline}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005}, in the Hopf
column, an \vocabk{orbit}{fnip-0015} that remains on the graph
#{n=n_{\infty}(V)} is an \vocabk{equilibrium}{fnip-0014}.}

\proof{
\p{The second component of that velocity vanishes on the graph.
Differentiating the formula for #{n_{\infty}} in \ref{fnip-0005} gives
##{\frac{d n_{\infty}}{d V}
   =\frac{1}{2V_{4}\cosh^{2}((V-V_{3})/V_{4})}.}
In the Hopf column #{V_{4}=30}, so this derivative is positive at
every #{V}. At a point of the graph other than an equilibrium, the
velocity is horizontal and nonzero, so its slope in the #{(V,n)}
plane is #{0}. The slope of the graph is positive. If a solution
#{(V(t),n(t))} starts at such a point, the derivative of
#{n(t)-n_{\infty}(V(t))} equals #{-(dn_{\infty}/dV)\,\dot V}, which
is nonzero. The solution leaves the graph, so an orbit that remains
on the graph is an equilibrium.}
}
